sights into Action Functional Decision Theory (A-FDT), particularly in the context of the Twin Prisoner's Dilemma. I'm focusing on Section 3 and Section 5, comparing the A-FDT's cooperative behavior in the \"Cooperate ($1M)\" scenario against the Conditional Decision Theory's \"Defect ($1k).\" The concept of \"computational twins\" is key here – independent executions of the same model. I'm noting the implications of shared settings and reasoning levels for each twin within this specific framework.\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9040,"completion_tokens":594,"total_tokens":9634,"cost":0.0090075,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.0090075,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.00678,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0022275},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":593,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}